WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.320
Narrator: Tails are like opinions.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.060
Basically, everyone has them: fish, birds,

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.330
most reptiles, and even some
of our closest relatives.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.963
So why are we missing out?

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.820
Since tails first evolved at
least 500 million years ago,

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.930
they've taken on every role imaginable.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.260
Geckos use them to store fat.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.940
Birds use them to steer through the air,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.570
and rattlesnakes use them
to scare off predators.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.010
But for most mammals, they
serve one major purpose:

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.360
balance.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.270
Yet, as you get closer to
humans on the evolutionary tree,

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.810
tails disappear. Gorillas don't have them,

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.170
and neither do chimps or any other apes,

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.090
including us, of course.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.200
To understand why, take
a look at how we walk.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.500
Some of us primates crouch
with our chest held diagonally

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.040
to the ground. Others
like gibbons and humans

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.330
can walk completely upright.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:05.050
Now, walking like this
gives us a huge advantage

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.060
because unlike four-legged animals,

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:11.030
which have to pour energy
into every step they take,

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.750
two legs take advantage of gravity,

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.200
which does some of the work for us.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.760
You see, each time we take a step,

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.150
gravity pulls us forward.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.190
The end result is that when we walk,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:28.190
we use around 25% less energy
than walking on all fours.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.570
And in the wild, every
ounce of energy you save

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:36.880
can mean the difference between
survival and starvation.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.850
But this way of getting
around also totally

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.150
eliminates the need for a tail

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.770
because even though a human head

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.280
weighs a hefty 5 kilograms,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.360
it sits on top of the body when you walk,

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.040
not in front, so you don't need
a tail as a counterbalance.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.360
Pretty disappointing, huh?

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.950
That being said, you
can still see a reminder

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.420
of a time when our ancient
primate ancestors had one.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.120
Just look at a human spine.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.170
You can see how the last few bones

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.490
are partially fused together.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.170
That's your tailbone.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.339
It's all that's left of our tail,

00:02:14.339 --> 00:02:19.339
and, yes, it's sad and
pathetic, and you can't wag it.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.240
Now, in rare cases, babies are born

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.210
with what looks like a tail,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.300
but that's not what's really going on.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.680
Most often, these tails
are actually tumors,

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.820
cysts, or even a parasitic twin.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.670
Even more occasionally, they're a true

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.710
outgrowth of the spine but
are completely boneless,

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.750
a soft tube made entirely
of fat and tissue.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.440
These types of tails usually
form as a birth defect,

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.120
a deformity of the spine
called spina bifida.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.040
And in these situations,
doctors will surgically

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.690
remove the tails with no harm to the baby.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.960
But as cool as it might
sound to have an extra limb

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.670
to swing through the trees
or keep mosquitoes away,

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.743
we are who we are today
because, well, we don't.

